# Line endings. Checklist item 37, added 2026-10-17.
#
# Why: the working tree had drifted into a mix - src/test/App.test.tsx, InputBox.test.tsx,
# contextEntry.test.ts and loopCompaction.test.ts were CRLF while SelectList.test.tsx, loop.test.ts
# and package.json were LF, and compaction.test.ts was internally mixed (263 CRLF lines and 9 bare
# LF). The committed blobs were already consistent, because core.autocrlf=true normalises on
# commit, so this broke nothing at runtime - but it cost real time: an ending-naive edit script
# rewrote one file's endings wholesale and produced a diff that hid the actual change inside it.
# One declared answer per file type means a script has something to rely on.

# Let git decide what is text; normalise it to LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than a bare `text=auto` - a deliberate departure from this item's original
# sketch. `text=auto` alone normalises the committed blob but leaves the checked-out file to each
# machine's core.autocrlf, which on Windows means CRLF. That is how the drift happened in the first
# place, and it keeps every `git add` printing "LF will be replaced by CRLF" for files that are
# already LF on disk. Pinning LF makes the declaration total: one answer per file type, the same on
# every platform, and the same as what these files already are. Nothing in this toolchain cares -
# node, tsc, tsx and Ink are all ending-agnostic - and the two cases that genuinely need CRLF are
# overridden below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files must be CRLF in the working tree. Not a style preference: cmd.exe mis-parses
# `call` and `goto` labels in an LF-only .bat and fails *silently*, which already happened once in
# this project. None are tracked right now; this is here so the first one added is correct without
# anyone having to remember why.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Patch files stay LF whatever the platform. patch-package parses these against LF sources, and a
# CRLF-converted patch fails to apply - a failure mode this repo has already been bitten by once
# (the ink 8.0.0 patch shipped carrying a corrupted literal and only surfaced at launch).
*.patch text eol=lf

# Shell scripts stay LF: a CRLF shebang line makes the kernel look for an interpreter whose name
# ends in \r and the script dies with a confusing "not found".
*.sh text eol=lf

# Compiled tree-sitter grammars are binary and must never be touched. `* text=auto` above would
# rely on git's NUL-byte heuristic to work this out; WASM starts with a `\0asm` magic so the
# detection does fire, but 25 MB of parsers is not something to leave to a heuristic. An
# LF-normalised grammar fails to load with an opaque emscripten error - the same symptom as an
# ABI-incompatible build, which cost real time to diagnose once already.
*.wasm binary

# Tree-sitter query files are ordinary text and want the repository default (LF), stated so the
# pairing with *.wasm above is obvious to the next reader.
*.scm text eol=lf
